  - [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m — no dependencies, no test script, no lint config
  - Two different runtimes, so "the app" is ambiguous, and "production" means different things in each

  One flag before we go further: [38;5;153mapp.js:5[39m already logs a username right next to a password variable. Any logging design
  here needs a redaction rule, not just a log call — I'll come back to that.

  [1mThe fork — which surface gets the logger.[0m This decides almost everything downstream: a browser logger can't write to a
  file or stderr and is useless for production debugging unless logs are shipped somewhere; a Node logger is the
  opposite, stdout is free and a remote sink is optional. Trying to serve both means an interface with two transports
  behind it.

  - [1mBrowser only[0m — matches where the real behavior is (the login flow), but forces the "where do logs go" question
    immediately; console-only logging in a user's browser is invisible to you in production. Hard to change later if you
    pick a browser-specific library.
  - [1mNode only[0m — cheapest useful thing: structured lines to stdout, your platform collects them. But [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m is a
    stub that prints a greeting; there's nothing there to debug.
  - [1mBoth, one shared module[0m — one [38;5;153mlog.debug()[39m API, transport chosen per environment. Costs a small abstraction layer up
    front and is the only option that stays cheap if you later add the other surface.

  I'd recommend [1mboth via one shared module[0m, because the code you actually want to debug is the browser flow, but the
  abstraction is a few dozen lines and picking one surface now is the expensive thing to undo.
